    "pov_path": ".security-pipeline/pov/PovPathTraversal.java",
    "regression_hint": "Invariant a correct fix must enforce: for every path handed to FileResourceManager.getResource(String p) (and any equivalent resource-manager implementation), the File resolved from `base` + the caller-supplied relative path must have a canonical path that is exactly `base` or starts with `base + File.separator`; any resolution that escapes must return null (or throw) before a Resource/FileResource is constructed. This check must happen at (or immediately after) FileResourceManager.java's `new File(base, path)` call (currently ~line 89), not only earlier \"canonicalization\" of the incoming string, since:\n - CanonicalPathUtils.canonicalize() must also normalize/reject backslash ('\\\\') as a path-separator-equivalent on all platforms (not just rely on OS-specific File separator behavior), otherwise Windows deployments remain exploitable even when callers \"pre-canonicalize\" via ServletContextImpl.getRealPath.\n - DefaultServlet.doGet's getPath(req) path (both the INCLUDE_* attribute variant and the plain getPathInfo()/getServletPath() variant) must not be allowed to reach FileResourceManager.getResource without going through the same containment check, since it currently performs zero canonicalization before calling the resource manager.\n\nKnown bypass variants the fix must also block (test each, not just the plain \"/../\" case exercised by this POV):\n - Plain forward-slash traversal: \"/../secret.txt\", \"/a/../../secret.txt\" (exercised here \u2014 must remain blocked).\n - Backslash-delimited traversal on Windows: \"..\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\", \"/a\\\\..\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\" (bypasses CanonicalPathUtils since it only special-cases '/').\n - Mixed separators: \"/a/..\\\\../secret.txt\".\n - URL-encoded / double-encoded dot-segments arriving through the servlet path-info or dispatcher INCLUDE_PATH_INFO/INCLUDE_SERVLET_PATH request attributes (trace 3), since those bypass normal URL decoding/normalization performed earlier in the pipeline.\n - Absolute-path style payloads on Windows such as \"C:\\\\..\\\\secret.txt\" or UNC paths (\"\\\\\\\\host\\\\share\\\\...\") that java.io.File may honor as absolute, bypassing the `base` prefix entirely regardless of \"..\" handling.\n - Paths that exist under `base` as a symlink pointing outside `base` (not exercised by this POV, but relevant if the fix relies solely on lexical \"..\" stripping rather than actual canonical-path containment).",
